Draw the screen-filling samples on a round watch.

WHY: these 26 samples carry their own `@Preview` upstream, and a bare one is device-less — so the
Wear retarget measures them against the 227dp watch screen with both axes wrapped, and each comes
out a 227dp SQUARE. The other 60 screen-filling samples reach this repo through generated
wrappers, which `scripts/samples-previews.mjs` gives the same device; this patch is only for the
ones the generator cannot touch because upstream already annotated them.

A square is wrong for a sample that fills the screen, in two ways that compound:

  - it shows pixels a watch never draws. `ScaffoldSample`'s bottom row spans x=45..355 of 454,
    where a round bezel exposes only x=149..305 at that height.
  - `isRound` is LOAD-BEARING, not cosmetic. `androidx.wear.compose.foundation.ResourcesKt` calls
    `Configuration.isScreenRound()`, so components branch on screen shape: a square canvas can lay
    a sample out differently from the watch it ships on, which makes the published sticker a claim
    about the API that the device would not honour.

Only the screen-filling ones. A sample that is one component still renders device-less and crops to
its own bounds — pinning those to a device is the regression the plugin's `retargetWearStickers`
KDoc already records, where a `FilledButton` sticker "became a 454x454 watch canvas with the button
adrift in the corner". Which samples are which is MEASURED off a render rather than inferred from
the sources, by `scripts/samples-device-bound.mjs`; its header says why a static rule over the
source is not good enough.

The device is the 225dp round spec `:catalog` uses for one of its five full-screen breakpoints, and
the black face is the same `@CatalogFullScreenModes` carries. ONE device rather than five: the kit
sheet renders every screen size because it reproduces a kit cell at each, while a sample
demonstrates how an API is called and says nothing about responsive behaviour.

A renderer-context fix rather than an upstream bug: a bare `@Preview` is right for a sample read in
Studio by someone who picks their own device, and under-specified for one captured as a still.

